015), in Bornholm (Ptychagnostus punctuosus zone; Rudolph 1994), France (Thoral 1935), Germany (Wurm 1925), the Czech Republic (Eccaparadoxides pusillus zone; e. g., Šnajdr 1990; Fletcher et al. 2005), and Spain (Pardailhania and Solenopleuropsis zones; Liñan & Gozalo 1986).","","text","",""]]},"LJPC":{"d":[["China (Yunnan)","","text","",""]]},"LK5FW":{"d":[["Norway","NO","iso","",""]]},"LNHG":{"d":[["","MXS","tdwg","",""]]},"MP3WB":{"d":[["North America","","text","",""]]},"MVTR":{"d":[["DZ","DZ","iso","",""],["MA","MA","iso","",""]]},"MZLV":{"d":[["Australia (Victoria, New South Wales)","","text","",""]]},"MZM5":{"d":[["Australia (New South Wales)","","text","",""]]},"NCSYL":{"d":[["Distribution: Mexico, Grenada and Venezuela. (Fig. 48) Material examined: MEXICO: ♀ Chiapas, Escuintla, Crawford. Lasiochilus basalis Reuter, B. Poppius det. (compared with type) ZMHF; GRENADA: ♀ Chantilly Est. (Windward side), H. H. Smith, 72. USNM. Discussion: As with the previous species, the author had no males with which study the genital structures. However, he left them in Dilasia because of the smooth dorsal surface, shape of pronotum, antennal morphology and the typical forefemora. This species is closely related to D. varicolor in having the second antennal segment thicker at apex, but differs by having this segment longer and by its darker coloration. Its ostiolar peritreme opening has the apex thickened as in D. punctipennis but in this latter species, the apex is acute: in D. basalis it is rounded.","","text","",""]]},"ND38L":{"d":[["Afrotropic","","text","native",""]]},"P2LSH":{"d":[["Sweden","SE","iso","native",""]]},"P3RT":{"d":[["Colombia (Amazonas); Peru; Bolivia (Beni); N-Brazil (Roraima, Amazonas, Acre); WC-Brazil (Mato Grosso)","","text","",""]]},"P7MC2":{"d":[["Norway","NO","iso","",""],["Norway","NO","iso","",""]]},"PBTC":{"d":[["","SOA","tdwg","",""]]},"PRQHN":{"d":[["Norway","NO","iso","",""]]},"QGGJ":{"d":[["Dakar","8950","mrgid","",""],["European waters (ERMS